# Testing and Verification

- For production behavior: write one focused failing test, observe the expected failure, implement minimally, then refactor while green.
- Test through module interfaces. Prefer deterministic fakes over mocks of implementation details.
- Every bug fix includes a regression test that fails without the fix.
- Unit tests must not touch the live desktop assignment, login-item registry, Apple wallpaper store, user library, or network.
- System adapters use temporary directories, fixture manifests, injected notifications, clocks, players, displays, and process samples.
- Lock-screen automation runs only against copied fixtures; live verification is explicit and reversible.
- Concurrency tests synchronize on events or clocks, never arbitrary sleeps.
- A passing narrow test is not proof of a successful build; run the affected suite and build before completion claims.
- Generated project changes require regeneration plus Debug and Release bundle verification.
- Record performance hardware, macOS build, media profile, display topology, sample duration, median, and p95.
- Do not weaken, skip, or delete a failing test merely to make CI green.
- Completion requires fresh command output, not expectation or prior agent reports.
